[[1035,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[2280,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[2367,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[3686,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[3698,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[8384,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[9796,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[10051,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[17203,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[18709,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]"],[11977,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]\n\n[[foundationsofmath.pdf]]"],[12001,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[FoundationsOfComputation_2.3.2.pdf]]\n\n[[foundationsofmath.pdf]]"],[4907,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[foundationsofmath.pdf]]"],[12661,"[[essentialsofmodernalgebra.pdf]]\n\n[[foundationsofmath.pdf]]"],[21175,"[[Euclidean Geometry Theorems]] organizes plane reasoning as definitions and proofs. [[Euclidean Axiomatic Geometry]] makes assumptions explicit, after which basic results such as equality of [[Vertical Angles]] and the [[Triangle Angle Sum]] support longer deductions. The [[Isosceles Triangle Theorem]] and [[Triangle Congruence]] transfer equality among corresponding parts. The [[Pythagorean Theorem]] links right triangles to squared lengths, [[Parallelogram Theorems]] organize parallel quadrilaterals, and [[Similar Triangles]] preserve shape under scaling. Circle geometry culminates in the [[Inscribed Angle Theorem]] and the supplementary-angle property of a [[Cyclic Quadrilateral]].\n\n[[Spherical Trigonometry and Spatial Vectors]] shows which plane intuitions survive in three dimensions and which must change. A [[Plane Equation in Three Dimensions]] is controlled by its normal vector, and a [[Sphere Equation]] fixes distance from a center. A [[Great Circle]] is cut by a plane through that center; three of its arcs bound a [[Spherical Triangle]]. The [[Cross Product]] produces a perpendicular vector and area, while the [[Scalar Triple Product]] produces oriented volume. Normals determine the [[Angle Between Planes]], making vector methods suitable for spherical angles. The [[Spherical Law of Cosines]] and [[Spherical Law of Sines]] solve general spherical triangles, and [[Napier's Rules]] provide a compact method for right spherical triangles.\n\nTogether, these subjects describe a progression from quantity to structure, from structure to representation, and from representation to change. Algebra makes transformations accountable, coordinates make geometry computable, functions make dependence explicit, limits control approximation, derivatives describe local variation, and geometry keeps the symbolic results tied to space. The recurring principle is preservation: every valid calculation or proof must preserve the relationship being studied while expressing it in a form better suited to the next question."],[21264,"[[Exponents and Roots]] gives concise notation for repeated multiplication and its reversal. In a power, the base is the repeated factor and the exponent records how many copies occur. The laws of exponents follow from this meaning: multiplying powers with a common base adds exponents, division subtracts them when the base is nonzero, a power raised to a power multiplies exponents, and a power of a product distributes the exponent across its factors.\n\nZero and negative exponents extend the pattern consistently. A nonzero base to the zero power equals one, while a negative exponent denotes the reciprocal of the corresponding positive power. Roots reverse exponentiation: a square root is a number whose square gives the radicand, and a cube root is a number whose cube does so. The notation is compact, but parentheses remain crucial because the square of a negative number differs from the negative of a square."],[21267,"[[Factoring and Algebraic Identities]] reverses expansion by expressing a sum as a product. The first step is often to remove the greatest factor common to every term. When no factor spans the full expression, grouping can expose a repeated binomial factor. Quadratic trinomials can be factored by finding numbers whose product and sum match the required coefficients.\n\nSpecial identities make recurring structures immediately recognizable. The square of a sum or difference produces two square terms and a doubled product. A difference of squares factors into conjugate binomials. Sums and differences of cubes use a linear factor paired with a quadratic factor. These identities support exact mental calculations, multi-stage factorization, and equation solving. A complete factorization may require applying more than one pattern in sequence."],[822,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[825,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[1668,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[1680,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[3479,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[3617,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[3815,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[4136,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[4370,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[4373,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[4442,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[4646,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[6188,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[6206,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[6230,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[6305,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[6326,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[7724,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[8141,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[8833,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9166,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9526,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9529,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9532,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9535,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9538,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9541,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9544,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9547,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9550,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9553,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[9667,"[[fakephotos.epub]]"],[